-- National Insurance Law, s 67 — counting children.
-- Quotations (`src:N`) are generated from line N of the deposited source.
--
-- WHAT THIS SECTION DOES. (a) A child is counted, at any one time, with one insured parent
-- only. (b) Two rules choose the parent: a child with two parents goes in the insured father's
-- count unless the child is with the mother only; a child with a natural parent and another
-- parent, both insured, goes in the count of the one the child is with.
--
-- HOW THIS ROW READS IT (NOTES.md, forks F7-F12). The candidates are the child's parents for
-- this Chapter (s 65) who are insured on the day. No candidate: the child is in no count. One
-- candidate: that parent's count, since there is no one to choose between (F7). Two: the first
-- limb when both are the child's own parents (natural or adoptive) and one is the father and
-- the other the mother (F8); the second limb when one is a natural parent and the other is a
-- step-parent or a person who supported the child (F8). Every other case is declined by name:
-- the text does not say where the child goes (F9-F11).
--
-- WHAT THE SECTION IS SUBJECT TO, AND IS NOT ENCODED. s 69A applies "notwithstanding anything
-- elsewhere in this Mark" to an insured man with children by more than one woman, and s 71
-- says what is paid by virtue of a parent who died or ceased to be insured. Both reach cases
-- this section would otherwise answer, so each is a named refusal, consulted before the
-- section answers.
--
-- THE ORDER OF THE COUNT. s 68 and s 1 speak of "the first child", "the fourth child" in the
-- parent's count; the text does not say how the count is ordered. This row orders it by date
-- of birth, eldest first; children born on the same day keep the order of the family's list
-- (fork F4).

